## Dependency Injection <a name="di"></a>

### DI Concept & Benefits

**What is Dependency Injection (DI)?**
Instead of a class creating its dependencies, they are provided (injected) from outside.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD: Tight coupling (ProductService creates dependencies)
public class ProductService
{
    private readonly DatabaseConnection _db = new DatabaseConnection();
    private readonly EmailService _email = new EmailService();
    
    public async Task CreateProductAsync(Product product)
    {
        _db.SaveProduct(product);
        _email.SendNotification("New product created");
    }
    
    // Problems:
    // - Can't unit test without real database
    // - Can't change implementation easily
    // - Hard to mock dependencies
}

// ✅ GOOD: Loose coupling (Dependencies injected)
public interface IProductRepository
{
    Task SaveAsync(Product product);
}

public interface INotificationService
{
    Task SendAsync(string message);
}

public class ProductService
{
    private readonly IProductRepository _repository;
    private readonly INotificationService _notification;
    
    // Constructor Injection
    public ProductService(
        IProductRepository repository,
        INotificationService notification)
    {
        _repository = repository;
        _notification = notification;
    }
    
    public async Task CreateProductAsync(Product product)
    {
        await _repository.SaveAsync(product);
        await _notification.SendAsync("New product created");
    }
    
    // Benefits:
    // - Can pass mock objects for testing
    // - Easy to swap implementations
    // - Follows SOLID principles
}
```

### Three DI Lifetimes Explained

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// ============================================
// 1. TRANSIENT (New instance EVERY TIME)
// ============================================
builder.Services.AddTransient<IReport, DailyReport>();

// Usage scenario:
// Request 1: controller gets instance A
// Request 1: same controller gets instance B (different!)
// Request 2: controller gets instance C (different!)

// When to use:
// - Stateless objects (no shared data)
// - Examples: Formatters, Validators, Mappers, Utilities
// - Cheap to create
public class DateFormatter
{
    // No state = safe to use transient
    public string Format(DateTime date) => date.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd");
}

public class ValidationRules
{
    // Stateless utility
    public bool IsValidEmail(string email) => email.Contains("@");
}

// ============================================
// 2. SCOPED (New instance per HTTP request)
// ============================================
builder.Services.AddScoped<IUserService, UserService>();

// Usage scenario:
// Request 1: Gets instance A
//   - Controller gets A
//   - Repository gets A
//   - Service gets A
// Request 2: Gets instance B (different!)
//   - Controller gets B
//   - Repository gets B

// When to use:
// - Request-specific state
// - Database connections/transactions
// - Unit of Work pattern
// - Examples: DbContext, Unit of Work, Services

public class ApplicationDbContext : DbContext
{
    // Each request gets its own DbContext
    // Ensures no data leakage between requests
    public DbSet<Product> Products { get; set; }
}

public class UserRepository : IUserRepository
{
    private readonly ApplicationDbContext _context;
    
    public UserRepository(ApplicationDbContext context)
    {
        _context = context;  // Scoped: Same instance within request
    }
}

// ============================================
// 3. SINGLETON (One instance for ENTIRE APPLICATION)
// ============================================
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ICache, MemoryCache>();

// Usage scenario:
// Request 1: Gets instance A
//   - Controller gets A
//   - Service gets A
// Request 2: Gets SAME instance A
//   - Controller gets A
//   - Service gets A
// Request 3: Gets SAME instance A

// When to use:
// - Shared state across all requests
// - Thread-safe implementation required!
// - Examples: Configuration, Logging, Caching, Connection Pools

public class AppConfiguration : IAppConfiguration
{
    // Singleton: Shared by all requests
    // Content doesn't change, only read operations
    public string GetApiKey() => "secret-key-123";
    public int GetMaxConnections() => 100;
}

public class ServiceCache : ICache
{
    // Singleton: Cached data shared across requests
    // MUST be thread-safe!
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, object> _cache = new();
    
    public void Set(string key, object value)
    {
        _cache[key] = value;  // Thread-safe operation
    }
    
    public object Get(string key)
    {
        _cache.TryGetValue(key, out var value);
        return value;
    }
}
```

