## Authentication & Authorization <a name="auth"></a>

### Authentication vs Authorization

**Authentication:** Who are you?
- User provides credentials (username/password)
- System verifies identity
- Creates session/token
- Example: Login process

**Authorization:** What can you do?
- Check user's permissions/roles
- Allow/deny access to resources
- Example: Only admins can delete products

### Cookie-Based Authentication (Traditional Web Apps)

**Flow:**
```
1. User submits login form
2. Server validates credentials
3. Server creates cookie with user info
4. Browser stores cookie
5. Browser sends cookie with each request
6. Server reads cookie to identify user
```

```csharp
// ============================================
// SETUP IN PROGRAM.CS
// ============================================

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register authentication
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddCookie(options =>
    {
        options.LoginPath = "/Account/Login";           // Redirect here if not authenticated
        options.AccessDeniedPath = "/Account/AccessDenied";
        options.ExpireTimeSpan = TimeSpan.FromDays(7);  // Cookie lifetime
        options.SlidingExpiration = true;               // Reset timer on each request
    });

// Register authorization policies
builder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("AdminOnly", policy =>
        policy.RequireRole("Admin"));
    
    options.AddPolicy("CanEdit", policy =>
        policy.RequireClaim("Permission", "Edit"));
});

// Add middleware in order
app.UseAuthentication();   // Identify user
app.UseAuthorization();    // Check permissions

