**Query Execution:**

```csharp
// LINQ Query (C#)
var activeProducts = _context.Products
    .Where(p => p.IsActive && p.Price > 100)
    .OrderBy(p => p.Name)
    .ToListAsync();

// Translates to SQL:
SELECT * FROM Products
WHERE IsActive = 1 AND Price > 100
ORDER BY Name

// Executes on database
// Results mapped back to Product objects
// Returned as List<Product>
```

**Lazy vs Eager Loading:**

```csharp
// ❌ LAZY LOADING (Problem: N+1 Query Problem)
var products = await _context.Products.ToListAsync();

foreach (var product in products)
{
    Console.WriteLine(product.Category.Name);  // ← Executes separate query per product!
}
// Total Queries: 1 + N (N = number of products) = SLOW!

// ✅ EAGER LOADING (Solution: Load related data upfront)
var products = await _context.Products
    .Include(p => p.Category)  // Load categories in same query
    .ToListAsync();

foreach (var product in products)
{
    Console.WriteLine(product.Category.Name);  // No additional queries
}
// Total Queries: 1 = FAST!

// ✅ EXPLICIT LOADING (Alternative)
var product = await _context.Products.FirstOrDefaultAsync(p => p.Id == 1);
await _context.Entry(product)
    .Reference(p => p.Category)
    .LoadAsync();  // Load category when needed
```

### 4. Asynchronous Programming

**Synchronous (Blocking):**

```csharp
// ❌ BLOCKING - Thread waits for I/O
public string GetUserData()
{
    Thread #1: Request → [waiting] → Response → Return
    (Thread stuck during wait, can't handle other requests)
}

// With 10 concurrent requests:
// Needs 10 threads, high memory usage, poor scalability
```

**Asynchronous (Non-blocking):**

```csharp
// ✅ NON-BLOCKING - Thread released during I/O
public async Task<string> GetUserDataAsync()
{
    Thread #1: Request → [released] 
    ↓
    (Thread handles other requests)
    
    Async I/O completes → [resumed on available thread]
    
    Response → Return
}

// With 10 concurrent requests:
// Can use 1-2 threads, low memory usage, better scalability
```

**Pattern:**

```csharp
// Always use async for I/O operations
public async Task<Product> GetProductAsync(int id)
{
    // Database query (I/O) - use async
    return await _context.Products.FindAsync(id);
}

public async Task<string> FetchDataAsync(string url)
{
    // HTTP request (I/O) - use async
    return await _httpClient.GetStringAsync(url);
}

public async Task SendEmailAsync(string email, string message)
{
    // SMTP send (I/O) - use async
    await _smtpClient.SendMailAsync(email, message);
}

// Don't use async for CPU-bound operations
public string CalculateHash(string data)
{
    // This is CPU-bound, no need for async
    return SHA256.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data)).ToString();
}
```

